libdrm on LikeOS: the platform branch.

libdrm knows two kinds of system: the one whose kernel it was written for,
recognised by __linux__, and "one of the BSDs", which is everything else.
The compiler driver here defines __LIKEOS__ and undefines __linux__, so the
BSD branch was taken: include/drm/drm.h wants <sys/ioccom.h> (the BSD
spelling of the ioctl macros) and every sysfs-style lookup in xf86drm.c --
how a descriptor is traced back to its device node, its PCI function and
its render node -- compiles out, leaving drmGetDevice2() with nothing to
say.

This system's kernel serves the same paths the first kind of system does
(/sys/dev/char/MAJ:MIN/device/..., /sys/bus/pci/devices/..., /proc), and
its <sys/ioctl.h> carries the conventional _IOC encoding.  So the header
gets a branch of its own, and every `#ifdef __linux__' in xf86drm.c and
xf86drmMode.c is widened to `|| defined(__LIKEOS__)': the code behind them
is exactly what runs here.

diff -Nur a/include/drm/drm.h b/include/drm/drm.h
--- a/include/drm/drm.h	2024-12-04 19:30:30.000000000 +0100
+++ b/include/drm/drm.h	2026-08-27 18:29:52.197718427 +0200
@@ -41,6 +41,24 @@
 #include <asm/ioctl.h>
 typedef unsigned int drm_handle_t;
 
+#elif defined(__LIKEOS__)
+
+/* LikeOS: fixed-width types from <stdint.h>, the conventional ioctl
+ * request encoding from <sys/ioctl.h>. */
+#include <stdint.h>
+#include <sys/ioctl.h>
+#include <sys/types.h>
+typedef int8_t   __s8;
+typedef uint8_t  __u8;
+typedef int16_t  __s16;
+typedef uint16_t __u16;
+typedef int32_t  __s32;
+typedef uint32_t __u32;
+typedef int64_t  __s64;
+typedef uint64_t __u64;
+typedef size_t   __kernel_size_t;
+typedef unsigned long drm_handle_t;
+
 #else /* One of the BSDs */
 
 #include <stdint.h>
